# FAILURE_PATTERNS.md

> Append-only registry of bug classes that shipped and failed. Every entry is a
> contract: check your change against this list before declaring it ready.
> Referenced by `CLAUDE.md`; reading it is mandatory before a ship.

## Recurring classes - watch these hardest

**Read this digest at session start.** It is not a summary of the registry; it
is the short list of classes that have bitten **more than once here**, which
makes them the bugs you are statistically about to write again today. The full
entries are below; these are the one-line reminders.

| Class | The reminder, in one line | class-guard |
|---|---|---|
| <F_SCOPE_NNN> | <ONE_LINE_REMINDER> | `<ASSERTION_NAME>` |
| <F_SCOPE_NNN> | <ONE_LINE_REMINDER> | `<ASSERTION_NAME>` |

Three rules keep the digest from becoming decoration:

- **Promotion is by recurrence, not by severity.** A class arrives here the
  second time it happens, whatever it cost either time. Severity picks what to
  fix first; recurrence picks what you are about to do again.
- **Classification is intake step one.** A new find is sorted into an existing
  class before anything else, and the immediate next question is **"what else
  is in that class right now?"** - that sweep finds live instances, which is
  why it is a step rather than an instinct.
- **Ask which class the new guard is itself an instance of.** A guard written
  in a hurry is code like any other, and the classes here apply to it.

### The `> class-guard:` convention

Every class in this digest carries, in its entry below, a line of the form:

```
> class-guard: <the assertion, by name, that fails when ANY member recurs>
```

It names a **machine-checkable assertion**, not a habit. CI requires that
guard to **exist**, to **run**, and to have a **mutation proving it can fail**
(`docs/TESTING_STANDARDS.md` section 4.1, the probe roster). A class-guard
nobody can watch go red is a sentence about intentions.

Adding a `> class-guard:` line to an existing entry is the **one** amendment
the append-only rule permits: it adds a defence without rewriting the record.

---

## Format

```
ID            stable, never reused: F-<SCOPE>-<NNN>
Title         one line
Discovered    when and where it first hurt
Symptom       what the failure looks like from outside
Root cause    the actual mistake
Check         the gate that catches the next one, automated where possible
Command       the exact command, when there is one
class-guard   for a recurring class: the named assertion that fails when any
              member of the class recurs (see the digest above)
```

Add an entry **and** the corresponding item in `SHIP_CHECKLIST.md` at the same
time. An entry with no check is a story, not a defence.

