## F-TEXTMATCH-STATE-001 - a reconcile matches free text instead of the anchor

- **Discovered:** <YYYY-MM-DD>, <PR_OR_ITEM>
- **Symptom:** A work item, ticket or record flips to done because its
  identifier was **mentioned** somewhere - in another change's description, in a
  comment, in a list of related work. Items deliberately parked get closed by
  unrelated activity.
- **Root cause:** The state-flipping matcher searched a free-text body for the
  identifier instead of reading the authoritative field. Any substring hit
  anywhere in the text was treated as the record's own state change.
- **Check:** State-flipping matchers anchor on the **authoritative field** - the
  structured id, the designated line, the API's own association - never a
  substring hit in a body. Where free text must be parsed, require the
  documented form at a documented position, and log every non-matching mention
  rather than acting on it.
- **Command:** `<STATE_RECONCILE_ANCHOR_TEST>`

---

## F-DUPDEF-001 - two live definitions of one thing, and the wiring picks one

- **Discovered:** <YYYY-MM-DD>, <PR_OR_ITEM>
- **Symptom:** A fix lands, the tests pass, and the behaviour does not change -
  because the fix landed in the copy nothing runs. Or two same-named classes,
  configs or constants drift until one surface disagrees with another for
  reasons nobody can locate.
- **Root cause:** A rename, a move, an extraction or a vendored copy left two
  definitions of one name alive. The wiring imports one, the interface or the
  test imports the other, and both are importable, so nothing errors.
- **Check:** **One definition per name.** On any rename or move, grep **every**
  import site in the same change and leave none pointing at the old path. Prefer
  **deleting the copy that can drift** over documenting that it must not -
  documentation does not survive the next person who finds the old name first.
- **Command:** `<GREP_DUPLICATE_DEFINITIONS>`

